Delirium (Terminal)
Palliative & End of Life
Acute fluctuating mental status, Inattention/disorientation, Visual hallucinations/delusions

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-018
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe hyperactive terminal agitation with florid hallucinations causing profound distress to the patient and family.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, adult daughter, son-in-law
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, sudden onset of severely fluctuating altered mental status and hyperactive psychomotor agitation. The patient was experiencing terrifying visual hallucinations, attempting to climb out of bed, and pulling at their subcutaneous lines. Urgent after-hours assessment and pharmacological intervention were absolutely required to ensure physical safety, relieve the profound psychological terror, and provide immediate support to the highly traumatized family prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Daughter states: "He suddenly didn't know who we were. He is terrified, pointing at the walls screaming about insects, and keeps trying to rip his pain pump out."
History of Present Illness: Over the last 24 hours, the patient has experienced an acute, abrupt onset of severely fluctuating altered mental status and profound cognitive impairment. There is severe disorientation to time, place, and eventually to closely familiar people. The family reports florid visual hallucinations, which are highly frightening and distressing in nature. The presentation is distinctly hyperactive, featuring severe agitation, pulling at lines, and continuous restless wandering when capable. Previously today, there was a hypoactive phase characterized by profound lethargy and somnolence. There is a complete reversal of the normal circadian sleep-wake cycle. The patient voices paranoid delusions and misinterprets the immediate clinical environment. Speech is incoherent, severely fragmented, and entirely nonsensical. This sudden deterioration explicitly correlates with the anticipated final days of life.
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely dependent, acutely agitated.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate risk of falls and self-injury from dislodging the continuous subcutaneous syringe driver.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/95 mmHg | HR: 122 bpm | Temp: 37.5 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly agitated, diaphoretic, picking at the air, and visibly terrified.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals severe global inattention, inability to track the examiner or follow single-step commands, and a highly fluctuating level of consciousness. Abdominal exam confirms the urinary catheter is patent and draining clear urine, ruling out a distended bladder. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the acute fluctuating mental status (alternating somnolence and agitation), severe inattention/disorientation (inability to focus or recognize family), and explicit visual hallucinations/delusions (picking at imaginary objects on the walls).
Environmental Observations: The room is chaotic. Bright overhead lights are on, and a loud television is playing, both of which are actively worsening the patient's sensory overload and paranoia.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is on a baseline opioid syringe driver; doses have not changed recently. No clear reversible pharmacological trigger identified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Delirium (Terminal) - Status: Exacerbated (Hyperactive Phase)
Differentials:

Acute urinary retention - Ruled out/unlikely explicitly due to the presence of a functioning, freely draining indwelling catheter and a soft, non-tender suprapubic region.

Opioid neurotoxicity - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as there is no prominent myoclonus, hyperalgesia, or recent rapid dose escalation, though it may be a minor contributing factor to the terminal cascade.

Severe untreated pain - Ruled out/unlikely to be the sole driver, as the patient does not wince or guard upon palpation or movement; the thrashing is purposeless and clearly driven by hallucinatory terror.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the terminal phase of their illness and is experiencing a severe, terrifying episode of hyperactive terminal delirium. The objective clinical assessment strictly confirms the acute, highly fluctuating nature of the severe inattention and florid hallucinations, classic for the dying brain. Because the patient is entirely unable to be reoriented and is actively attempting to self-harm by pulling out essential symptom-control lines, waiting for an in-hours review would subject the patient to unimaginable psychological agony and physically unsafe conditions. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was essential to definitively diagnose the terminal nature of the delirium, rapidly administer parenteral antipsychotics and sedatives, and optimize the sensory environment to restore safety and peace.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous midazolam and levomepromazine to achieve rapid behavioral control and relieve the terrifying hallucinations. Adjusted the baseline syringe driver to include a continuous infusion of haloperidol.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you notice the patient becoming restless and staring intently at an empty space (trigger), immediately dim the lights and speak in a low, slow, explicitly reassuring tone (behavior). This actively reduces the sensory input that the dying brain is misinterpreting, lowering the risk of a full panic cascade (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued community palliative care nursing support.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure all medical lines are taped securely and routed under the patient's clothing to remove them from their immediate visual field.
Follow-up: Community nursing review tomorrow morning to evaluate the efficacy of the new syringe driver dosages.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the severe agitation breaks through the new sedation, or if the patient manages to pull out the syringe driver needle, the family must immediately administer the prescribed breakthrough sublingual/buccal sedation and contact the 24/7 palliative care triage line for further instructions.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Reversing terminal delirium is rarely possible; the absolute goal of treatment is the total elimination of distress and fear, acknowledging this usually requires deep sedation.
Clinical Course: This hyperactive phase signals that the physiological dying process is actively occurring. The patient is likely in their final days or hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Family: Maintain a calm, softly lit, and quiet environment. Do not attempt to argue with or correct the patient's hallucinations; instead, validate their distress and reassure them of their safety.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: (To Family) As the body systems fail, the brain's chemistry becomes chaotic, causing these terrifying dreams while he is awake. He is not in physical pain, but he is terrified. The injections will calm this chemical storm, allowing him to sleep deeply and peacefully, completely removing the fear.
Actionable Steps: Turn off the television and loud overhead lights. Play soft, familiar music. Always approach him slowly from the front so you do not startle him.

